<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gongsun Hong</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/200-bce">200</a>–121 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Kung-sun Hung</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gongsun Hong was a senior official of the Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a> and, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dong-zhongshu">Dong Zhongshu</a>, one of the earliest proponents of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a> at the Han court. Born in Zichuan in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>, a former prison officer who had made his living tending pigs, he began his political career at sixty and rose from commoner to grand secretary in 126 and chancellor in 124 BCE, the first commoner and first Confucian chancellor and the first chancellor made a marquis. His support brought into being the national academy and the imperial examination, a precedent that lasted into the twentieth century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a> and Dong Zhongshu called him a flatterer and a hypocrite.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a>, Gongsun Hong had been a prison officer who, after his dismissal, worked as a farmhand tending pigs. His family was poor and he did not study the Spring and Autumn Annals much until he was forty. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Shiji</a> links him with the Guliang commentary on the Annals, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-han">Hanshu</a> stresses his interest in the Gongyang, which he reportedly studied under Huwu Zidu in the state of Qi. The Shiji rates his learning below that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dong-zhongshu">Dong Zhongshu</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He probably first set out his views in 134 BCE, after the death of the Daoistic Empress Dowager, in answer to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a>'s request for advice. His speech joined the Confucian values of humaneness, righteousness and moderation with ideas from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legalism">Legalism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohism">Mohism</a>: that able men should hold posts matching their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> and that the common people should be left free to farm. Sent as an envoy to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, he resigned when his opinion differed from the emperor's and thereafter rarely disagreed with him openly. He proposed that talented young men be trained at an imperial academy in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/four-books-and-five-classics">Five Classics</a> and given entry-level posts.</p><p>Working with the legal official Zhang Tang, whose policies he lent the authority of the classics, Gongsun helped to bring the government under tight central control. Together they introduced a law that punished those who knew of a crime and failed to report it, and they removed rivals such as Ji An. Gongsun also recommended the execution of Zhufu Yan, and Ban Gu holds him responsible for sending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dong-zhongshu">Dong Zhongshu</a> away to Jiaoxi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Gongsun Hong died of natural causes on 7 April 121 BCE, a year after the Huainan trials; his son inherited his rank but lost it in a trial. Judgements of him differ. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a> described a suspicious man, outwardly magnanimous but inwardly scheming, and Herrlee G. Creel saw him as largely a figurehead Confucian who justified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a>'s despotic rule. Griet Vankeerberghen credits him with promoting frugality, modesty and incorruptibility, and he was praised for giving most of his salary to fellow scholars. Ban Gu thought his ability outstanding, and Homer H. Dubs called him admirable in personal conduct.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gongsun Hong” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The founder Liu Bang was a commoner, and <b>Gongsun Hong</b>, a former pig-breeder, became Emperor Wu’s chancellor.</blockquote><a href="/societies/han-china">Social Hierarchy of Han China</a></li></ul></section>
